440:// ----------------------------------------------------------------------
6:// Every edit this backend makes to shader text before it reaches the compiler, in one
7:// place, with the evidence for each.
8://
9:// Nine live in this file. Four are inherited from the x64 DX9 build -- that build is the
10:// image this port is measured against, so its source patches are part of the target
11:// whether or not one likes them. The rest are new, and exist either because
12:// d3dcompiler_47 rejects constructs the June-2010 D3DX compiler accepted and offers no
13:// legacy switch to get them back, or because a D3D9 fixed-function stage has to become
14:// shader code.
15://
16:// Transforms 1 to 6 are enumerated below. The other three are documented at their
17:// implementations rather than here, because each is long enough to need its own argument:
18://
19://   7. functions.inc is served with the constants include prepended, so the pixel
20://      includes stop depending on being included in a particular order.
21://   8. The pixel epilogue -- the alpha test clip, and the fog blend D3D9 did in fixed
22://      function. Adds a FOG input to the entry point.
23://   9. A FOG output for the minority of vertex programs that lack one, which is what
24://      makes 8's input safe to declare universally.
25://
26:// THREE MORE TRANSFORMS LIVE OUTSIDE THIS FILE, and anything reasoning about the total set
27:// -- a precompiled bytecode cache's key, most obviously -- has to count those too:
28:// Direct3d11_VertexShaderData::parseHeader strips the `//hlsl` marker and the leading
29:// `#define` block (load-bearing: leaving the header in place makes seven vertex programs
30:// fail X4532) and synthesises the texture-coordinate tag macros, and the `point` rename is
31:// passed as a compiler macro rather than edited into the text.
104:// It does not translate assembly. A program whose first line is `//asm` or a bare
105:// `ps.1.x` version directive is not HLSL, D3DCompile has no assembler, and D3D9 shader
106:// assembly is not a D3D11 shader model. Ninety-seven such programs are reachable at

 succeeded in 1132ms:
12:// writing the pitch and pixel-data fields a lock has to report, so the name and the
13:// global namespace are both fixed by the engine.
14://
15:// ----------------------------------------------------------------------
16:// Uploads go through a per-lock scratch buffer, not a mapped resource
17://
18://   Cube maps are locked and written face by face at runtime while NOT being
19://   flagged dynamic, so a DYNAMIC resource -- which can only map its single
20://   subresource -- cannot be the general upload path. The first texture the client
21://   ever creates is a 128x128 DXT5 cube map with eight mip levels, which is 48
22://   (face, level) locks before the first frame.
23://
24://   The engine's pitch contract is its own: a lock reports the pitch of the format
25://   the CALLER asked for, which is frequently not the format the resource is in,
26://   and the loader collapses a whole mip level into a single file read when that
27://   pitch matches the file's. A tight scratch buffer honours that exactly; a mapped
28://   staging row pitch cannot, and would silently take the slow row-by-row branch.
29://
30:// ----------------------------------------------------------------------
31:// Three formats, not one
32://
33://   m_nativeFormat   what getNativeFormat() reports. The engine compares a lock's
34://                    requested format against this to decide whether it is getting
35://                    raw access, and Graphics::setMouseCursor asserts on it.
36://
37://   m_storageFormat  the engine format whose row layout the resource actually has.
38://                    Differs from the native format only for TF_L_8, which is
39://                    stored expanded to TF_ARGB_8888 because DXGI has no L8 that
40://                    samples as (L, L, L, 1).
41://
42://   m_dxgiFormat     what the resource was created with.
43://
44:// Any lock whose requested format is not m_storageFormat goes through
45:// Direct3d11_TextureConverter in whichever direction the lock needs. DX9 did the
46:// same thing with a scratch surface and D3DXLoadSurfaceFromSurface; there is no
47:// D3DX in D3D11, so the conversion is ours.
48://
49:// ----------------------------------------------------------------------
50:// Read-back is tracked, not assumed
51://
52:// A lock that does not discard has to present the subresource's current contents:
53:// the mouse-cursor alpha hack read-modify-writes its texture, and
54:// computeRepresentativeColor reads a pixel out of one. But loadSurface ALSO passes
55:// discardContents = isDynamic(), which is false for every .dds, so a naive
56:// implementation would stage-copy and stall on every mip level of every texture at
57:// load time. DX9 pays nothing there, because a MANAGED surface locked without
