 succeeded in 1363ms:
6:// The shader constant register file, and the per-draw slice that must not live
7:// in it.
8://
9:// The setters below deliberately keep D3D9's signature -- a register index, a
10:// pointer, and a count of float4 rows -- because that is what lets the light
11:// manager and the static shader data port across with their upload code
12:// unchanged. What changes is everything behind them.
13://
14:// D3D9's setters had no CPU shadow, no comparison and no coalescing: every call
15:// went straight to SetVertexShaderConstantF, and the driver's register file made
16:// that cheap. D3D11 has no register file. A constant buffer is a resource, and
17:// updating one costs either a rename or a partial update, so the same call
18:// pattern issued naively becomes the dominant per-draw cost of the renderer.
19://
20:// So constants are shadowed here, marked dirty per row, and flushed at most once
21:// per stage per draw -- and the two matrices that genuinely change per draw are
22:// moved out of the register file entirely:
23://
24://   b0  $Globals      the register file, rows 0..95, flushed only when dirty
25://   b1  reserved      the pixel epilogue (alpha test reference, fog colour),
26://                     which arrives with the alpha test work
27://   b3  SwgPerObject  objectWorldCameraProjection and objectWorld, 128 bytes,
28://                     taken from a ring so a draw costs an offset, not an upload
29://
30:// Leaving the two matrices in b0 is what makes the arithmetic fail. They occupy
31:// c0..c7, so touching them dirties the front of the register file, and a
32:// per-draw flush of the reflected extent then moves on the order of 1.5 KB per
33:// draw where D3D9 moved 128 bytes. At a few thousand draws a frame that is
34:// megabytes of constant traffic per frame against DX9's hundreds of kilobytes.
35:// The corresponding asset change -- dropping the register(c0) and register(c4)
36:// annotations from vertex_shader_constants.inc and declaring those two matrices
37:// in a cbuffer at b3 -- lands with the shader corpus conversion. Until then this
38:// backend writes them to b3 and no shader reads them, which is visible as
26:	// Sized from the register enumeration rather than from a literal. The vertex
27:	// file has to reach c95 because registers.inc aliases four scalars onto it;
28:	// VSCR_MAX alone stops at 68 and would leave every one of those reading zero.
29:	int const cms_vertexRows = VSCR_CBUFFER_ROWS;
30:	int const cms_pixelRows  = PSCR_CBUFFER_ROWS;
31:
32:	// The rows the per-object ring owns. D3D9 put these two matrices at c0..c7 in
33:	// the register file; here they are a separate buffer, so the register file's
34:	// front eight rows are never touched per draw.
35:	int const cms_perObjectFirstRow = VSCR_objectWorldCameraProjectionMatrix;
36:	int const cms_perObjectRows     = 8;
37:	int const cms_perObjectBytes    = cms_perObjectRows * cms_bytesPerRow;
38:
39:	// Constant buffer offsets must be a multiple of sixteen constants.
40:	int const cms_perObjectStride   = 256;
41:	int const cms_perObjectSlices   = 1024;
42:
43:	float          *ms_vertexShadow;
44:	float          *ms_pixelShadow;
45:
46:	int             ms_vertexDirtyFirst;
47:	int             ms_vertexDirtyLast;
48:	int             ms_pixelDirtyFirst;
49:	int             ms_pixelDirtyLast;
50:
51:	ID3D11Buffer   *ms_vertexGlobals;
52:	ID3D11Buffer   *ms_pixelGlobals;
53:
54:	// The pixel epilogue at b1. One row, holding the alpha test as a (scale, bias) pair the
55:	// injected shader code applies with a single multiply-add and a clip. It is a separate
56:	// buffer rather than a row of $Globals because $Globals byte offsets ARE the register
57:	// ABI every shader in the corpus depends on, and there is no reason to move that
58:	// contract for something the backend owns outright.
298:void Direct3d11_ConstantBuffers::setVertexShaderConstants(int index, void const *data, int 
numberOfConstants)
299:{
300:	NOT_NULL(data);
301:	DEBUG_FATAL(index < 0 || numberOfConstants < 0, ("Direct3d11: bad vertex constant range %d + 
%d", index, numberOfConstants));
302:
303:	// A write past the end of the register file is fatal rather than clamped.
304:	// D3D9 FATALs on the same condition through FATAL_DX_HR, and a silent clamp
305:	// would drop constants the shader then reads as whatever was there before.
306:	FATAL(index + numberOfConstants > cms_vertexRows, ("Direct3d11: a vertex constant write of %d 
row(s) at register %d runs past the end of the %d-row register file.", numberOfConstants, index, 
cms_vertexRows));
307:
308:	if (!numberOfConstants)
309:		return;
310:
311:	int const bytes = numberOfConstants * cms_bytesPerRow;
312:	float * const destination = ms_vertexShadow + (index * 4);
313:
314:	// Only mark dirty when something actually changed. D3D9 had no shadow and so
315:	// no way to notice; here the comparison is far cheaper than the upload it
316:	// avoids, and the light manager re-sends identical blocks constantly.
317:	if (memcmp(destination, data, bytes) == 0)
318:		return;
319:
320:	memcpy(destination, data, bytes);
321:	markVertexDirty(index, numberOfConstants);
322:}
