24:// No transposes, and why that is correct rather than lucky
25://
26:// D3D9's fixed-function pipeline wanted matrices in one layout and its vertex shaders wanted
27:// the transpose, which is why Direct3d9.cpp carries an #ifdef FFP fork through every one of
28:// these functions and calls D3DXMatrixMultiplyTranspose on the FFP side. This backend is
29:// vertex-and-pixel-shader only, so only the VSPS half of each fork is ported -- untransposed
30:// throughout.
31://
32:// That layout survives into D3D11 untouched. The engine uploads rows, c0 being row 1, and the
33:// programs read them with dp4, which is a row dotted against the vertex. HLSL's default
34:// column-major packing plus D3DCOMPILE_ENABLE_BACKWARDS_COMPATIBILITY places register cN at
35:// $Globals byte offset 16N, so the bytes the engine writes are the bytes the program reads.
36:// Adding a transpose here, or compiling with PACK_MATRIX_ROW_MAJOR, would break it.

