18:// That is one system-memory copy per static buffer, which is exactly what
19:// D3DPOOL_MANAGED was already costing -- the memory is not new, it has simply
20:// become visible.
21://
22:// A note on what this deliberately does NOT do. The prior DX11 attempt also kept a
23:// shadow, but re-uploaded the WHOLE buffer with UpdateSubresource on every unlock
24:// regardless of whether the lock was writable, and kept the shadow uninitialised
25:// until first use. Immutable buffers, which most of these are, then paid a full
84: * USAGE_DEFAULT with UpdateSubresource rather than IMMUTABLE, even though most of
85: * these are written exactly once. IMMUTABLE would be marginally better for the
86: * write-once case and is impossible for the rest: the engine relocks static
87: * buffers to modify geometry -- terrain and skeletal paths do -- and an immutable
88: * resource cannot be updated at all, so it would have to be recreated, which
89: * costs an allocation inside a frame. DEFAULT is the honest choice for a buffer
90: * whose write count is not known at construction.
